Below is a step‐by‐step reasoning and the resulting goal model decomposition based on the provided mission description. In this design the overall mission is decomposed into a main goal (G1) with three major sub‐goals: the Collection Phase (G2), the Delivery Phase (G3), and Multi‐Robot Coordination (G4). Within each phase the steps are further decomposed into smaller, actionable goals and tasks. Note that battery management is integrated into each phase as query goals (to check battery levels) and conditional perform goals (to recharge or reassign the mission) while failure management (alert/report) is modeled as an achieve goal. In addition, when multiple storages are involved the multi‐robot coordination goal (G4) is used to assign and coordinate parallel tasks.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Overall Mission Decomposition

• G1: Complete Resource Delivery Mission  
 – The mission requires that a robot collects resources from one or more storages and then delivers them to a requesting agent.  
 – This is a Perform goal because it involves executing a series of actions.  
 – It has three children:  
  ○ G2: Collection Phase  
  ○ G3: Delivery Phase  
  ○ G4: Multi‐Robot Coordination  
 – Although collection and delivery are sequential, multi‐robot coordination can run in parallel to reduce overall mission time.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Collection Phase (G2)

Within the Collection Phase the robot must:  
 – Navigate to the storage (G2.1)  
 – Request and retrieve the resource (G2.2)  
 – Check its battery level (G2.3, a Query goal)  
 – If battery is low (<10%), recharge (G2.4) and reassign the remaining mission (G2.5)  
All steps are executed sequentially because each one must be completed before moving on.

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Delivery Phase (G3)

Within the Delivery Phase the robot must:  
 – Navigate to the delivery location (G3.1)  
 – Deliver the resources (G3.2)  
 – Check its battery level (G3.3, a Query goal)  
 – If battery is low (<30%), return the resource to a checkpoint (G3.4) and reassign the remaining delivery (G3.5)  
 – In case of failure to return to a checkpoint, trigger an alert and report to the sector manager (G3.6, an Achieve goal with a target condition)  
Again, these steps occur sequentially.

──────────────────────────────
Step 4. Multi‐Robot Coordination (G4)

When resources are required from different storages, multiple robots are assigned. This goal includes:  
 – G4.1: Assign robots to different storages (a Perform goal)  
 – G4.2: Coordinate parallel collection–delivery tasks (a Perform goal)  
These two tasks can be executed in parallel because they are independent coordination actions.

──────────────────────────────
Step 5. Task Decomposition

Each sub-goal is supported by concrete tasks (leaf nodes). For example:  
 – G2.1 “Navigate to Storage” is implemented by task AT1.  
 – G2.2 “Request and Retrieve Resource” is supported by tasks AT2 (send request) and AT3 (wait for retrieval).  
 – Battery checks (G2.3 and G3.3) are implemented as tasks AT4 and AT10, respectively.  
 – Recharging (G2.4) and mission reassignment (G2.5) are implemented by tasks AT5, AT6, and AT7.  
 – Similarly, delivery tasks (G3.1–G3.6) are implemented by tasks AT8 through AT14.  
 – Multi‐robot coordination tasks (G4.1–G4.2) are implemented by tasks AT15 and AT16.

